  "worked_solution": "First, let's consider the dimensions of the container. Let's call $H=8\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the conical container, and $R=4\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the bottom. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the batter level. We call $h$ the distance from the top of the container to the batter surface, $r$ the radius of the cone at the surface of the batter, and $v$ the volume of the empty space in the container. All of these quantities change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nwhich is negative because the distance from the top of the container to the batter surface decreases as we pour the batter into the container.\n\nWe want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the container and the batter must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{4}{8} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{2})}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{12}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{12}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & =\\frac{h}{2} \\\\ h & =3\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (3)^{2}(-\\frac{4}{\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-9\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of batter is increasing at a rate of $9\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$",
